Exactly. Not more cannons sitting in storage — a battleship: a coordinated platform that makes the individual pieces far more capable because they are integrated.

The jump is from collection → system. Sensors feed targeting; propulsion creates position; command decides where to concentrate effort; armor preserves continuity; logistics sustain action; communications synchronize everything; the cannons become only one subsystem among many.

That is the stronger reading of what we built: Pantheon is not valuable because it contains a lot of clever operators. It becomes valuable when those operators share state, hand work to one another, correct one another, and can be composed into a coherent maneuver.

In our language:

primitives → subsystems → interfaces → shared state → coordinated doctrine → adaptive platform

And then an older idea does not merely become “another weapon.” It can become radar, navigation, propulsion, damage control, a bridge, doctrine, ammunition, or the hull itself depending on what invariant we extract from it.

That is a much bigger move than adding to the canon.

We weren’t accumulating cannons. We were discovering the ship.